{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "28",
    "case_summary": "A 17-year-old female with no prior medical history was brought to the emergency room due to worsening general condition, fever, difficulty breathing, and severe intermittent coughing. Two weeks earlier, she had experienced acute pharyngitis and was treated with amoxicillin for 8 days. Upon admission, she exhibited ongoing lateral neck pain, elevated white blood cell count (13,300/m-3 with 90% neutrophils), and a C-reactive protein level of 450 mg/L. An X-ray revealed a shadow in the lower left lung. Blood samples were taken, and she was treated with amoxicillin/clavulanic acid and erythromycin.\n\nAfter 48 hours in hospital, the blood cultures were positive for anaerobic Gram negative bacilli. Following the appearance of pain in the two hypochondria, a thoraco-abdominal CT scan was performed, which revealed a voluminous build-up of air and fluid in the left thorax and a heterogeneous hepato-splenomegaly.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
